		    // and either the return value does not equal "Old_arrListIndexOfX" or "Old_arrListIndexOfX" is negative, then the behavior of this method is undefined.
            if (New_Ret >= 0)
            {
                Debug.Assert((Old_arrListContainsX && ((New_Ret == Old_arrListIndexOfX) || Old_arrListIndexOfX < 0)) == false);
            }
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ The Count of the old array list and the new array list is the same.
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ The index-of-x in the old array list equals the new return value or -1 if the old array list did not contain x.
            Debug.Assert((Old_arrListContainsX && (New_Ret == Old_arrListIndexOfX)) ||
                          (!Old_arrListContainsX && (New_Ret == -1)));
        }
    }
}
```
The test cases for LastIndexOf method are all constructed using the PexSettings. UseFuzzingLevel property set to 100 to reduce randomness and thus achieve a more consistent code coverage. For more information, see the MSDN documentation for PexSettings. UseFuzzingLevel property.

The PexObserve.ValueForViewing method is used in some test cases to pass the state of variables to the progress viewer. By inspecting the values in the progress viewer, you can validate the test cases.

Note: These test cases use assertions to ensure that the test is behaving as expected. The method in which these assertions are embedded should be instrumented and tested with tools such as PostSharp to observe the behavior of the assertion handler when the conditions in the assertions are not met.

Please note that the PexSettings and PexObserve.ValueForViewing can't be added in this simple example. They are included for your guidance and might not be suitable in your case.

This example demonstrates the usage of ArrayList in C# and its various methods. It tests the array list's behavior under various method calls.

You can add more test cases to test more methods and scenarios to increase the test coverage.

For more information about testing array list operations, you can refer to the following articles:

[Code Contracts: Understanding and Using](https://code.msdn.microsoft.com/windowsdesktop/Code-Contracts-Understanding-and-9bc57fe9)

[Using Pex and C# to test your .NET code on Windows](https://channel9.msdn.com/Series/Code-Contracts/Using-Pex-and-C-Sharp-to-test-your-NET-code-on-Windows)

[Unit Testing with Microsoft Pex and Visual Studio](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/unit-test-with-microsoft-pex?view=vs-2019) 

These steps are just a guideline and may not work perfectly for every situation. Your program might also contain other more complex methods which you could similarly test with the same methodology.
 
Remember, good coding practice involves testing, so writing tests for array operations is a good idea, but don't be afraid if you make a mistake. Write a few tests, run them, understand the error, then adjust to fix it.

Finally, if you use a version control system (like git), take advantages of it. This way, you can revert back any changes that may lead to a failure case.

Good luck with your project.



