        [PexExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentException))]
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfTest1([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList x, [PexAssumeNotNull]int[] a, [PexAssumeValid]int num)
        {
            Assume.AssumeTrue(num > 0);
            x.AddRange(a);
            bool ret = Convert.ToBoolean(x.LastIndexOf(num));
            Assert.IsTrue(ret);
        }
    } 
}
```

In the example above we added a test method where we were trying to find the index of a certain number in the array list and make sure it's returns a negative number. The other test methods were checking if the last index of the item is greater than the previous index.

Another point to note in the example above is the use of Assert methods – these methods allow for checking if a certain condition is met. In our case, we're using them to check if certain return values meet certain conditions. For example, "Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX || New_Ret == -1);". This line is checking if either old array list contains the value 'x', or the returned value is -1.

Finally, we also used `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` methods to provide values for viewing during unit testing, which will be helpful when debugging.

#### Exploring Other Collections with Pex

Pex can also test your code on other collections like List, Queue, Stack, HashSet, etc. You can do so by simply replacing ArrayList with other collections like List, Queue, Stack and HashSet.

#### Advanced Exploration

If you want more sophisticated exploration, you can use Attributes like `PexExplorationAttribute`, `PexInlineAttribute`, `PexAllowedExceptionFacts`, `PexUseExceptionFromTypeAttribute`, and more.

## When to use Pex

Pex (Program Exploration) is mostly useful when performing Unit Testing on complex code units/methods. It automatically generates a large number of test cases to cover various scenarios and edge cases to ensure the robustness of your system. It's especially handy when you have a complex or untested code section.

Keep in mind that Pex may not find every possible edge case or scenario where the normal tests would fail, and as such, it's a good practice to pair it with manual testing as well.

## Conclusion

.NET Pex is a very powerful tool that gives developers the potential to auto-generate unit tests for their code, in fact, it can substantially reduce the amount of manual effort required for writing these tests. Developers can just focus on writing code logic while Pex takes care of generating test cases.

## References

- [Finding Bugs with Pex](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/download/details.aspx?id=16479)

