A: Assuming that the test is just for the contract of the LastIndexOf method, here is how you might define it:
For any list of integers arrList and for any integer x there exists an element at index i such that arrList.GetRange(i, arrList.Count - i) == x from the point in time that List.Add(x) has been invoked.

This could be encoded as such:

[TestMethod, HostType("Moles")]
    public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeNonNull]ArrayList arrList, [PexPositiveTest]int x)
    {
        // The following line is necessary when you are using Moles
        PexMethodInvoker.SuppressDuringDynamicConstruction = true;

        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

        // Setup an Moles for Add method to capture new array list and x.
        ArrayList list = arrList;
        int xValue = x;

        // Moles for the Add method.
        int index = 0;
        ArrayList listAdd = null;
        int xAdd = 0;

        // Set up the Mole for the Add method.
        arrList.Add = (int xToAdd) =>
        {
            listAdd = list;
            xAdd = xToAdd;
        };

        // Call the method under test.
        arrList.Add(x);

        // Use assert to ensure the method's contract.
        Assert.IsTrue(listAdd == arrList);
        Assert.IsTrue(xAdd == xValue);
        Assert.IsTrue(arrList.LastIndexOf(x) >= index);

        // Increment the index to prepare for the next invocation.
        index++;
    }

The Moles framework by Microsoft is used to enable setup and teardown code for methods under test to be written in the "normal" managed code, while maintaining the semantics and side-effects of the methods under test.
This code assumes the use of Moles and does not run without it.

