A: I don't know if this would help solve your problem but it would be appropriate to use the IndexOf method with the count of the ArrayList as the starting position parameter. Like this:
int lastIndex = arrList.IndexOf(x, arrList.Count - 1);

This will start the search from the end of the list to find the last occurrence of x. The index returned is the one-based index if the found item and -1 if not found. 

Also, I assumed that you're looking for a method that will return the last index of an item in an array list without making multiple iterations over the array list. This IndexOf method, while being a two-pass linear operation having a time complexity of O(n), wouldn't run a loop to find the last occurrence.

Please let me know if this works for getting the last index of an item in an ArrayList.

A: You can get the last index without looping using System.Collections.ArrayList.BinarySearch method. 

The BinarySearch method searches for an element in a sorted ArrayList using binary search. If the element is not found in the ArrayList, a negative number that represents the bitwise complement of the next larger number of a signed integer is returned.

int LastIndexOf(ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    arrList.Sort();
    int index = arrList.BinarySearch(x);
    while (index < arrList.Count - 1 && (int)arrList[index+1] == x) 
    {
        index++;
    }
    return index > arrList.Count ? -1 : index;
}

The function returns the first index of element x in the sorted ArrayList. When a match is found, it iterates the list again to find subsequent occurrences. Then, it determines if the match occurred at the end of the list (i.e., the element is the largest in the ArrayList). If there is a match at the end, the found index is returned; otherwise, -1 is returned. 

Remember, BinarySearch works only on sorted list. So if your ArrayList is not sorted, you need to add arrList.Sort() before calling BinarySearch.

Please note that in the ArrayList.BinarySearch() search, the elements are indexed starting from 0, so it is necessary to ensure that when you return the index, if the value is found, you also need to subtract 1 from it since the index starts from index 0. So in the scenario where such a match is found, the index returned should be offset by one as compared to what you would expect if the ArrayList was 1-indexed.

Another tricky point to be aware of is that if this function is invoked with x equal to the minimum allowable value for the type of elements stored in the ArrayList, the returned value may be -1, which might not be what you expect. If the minimum value is an acceptable value in your context, then there is noting to be done here. But check this carefully.

One final point to consider is that the time complexity for BinarySearch is O(log n). So for large-sort List with performance critical operations, you should consider other options.

In essence, the BinarySearch operation might be slower than a loop, but with large list BinarySearch is guaranteed to find the item if it exists, and is much faster on sorted lists.

