A: It's not clear what you mean by 'Last Index Of'.  There is no built-in method for it in ArrayList. However, you can create your own method that iterates through the list in reverse order and stops at the first occurrence of the item. Here is a way to do something similar:
public int LastIndexOf(ArrayList list, object value)
{
    for (int i = list.Count - 1; i >= 0; i--)
    {
        if (list[i].Equals(value))
            return i;
    }

    return -1;--------------------            // and the last occurrence of x in the old array list is at index [New_arrListIndexOfX], then the return value is the last occurrence of x in the old array list minus its count.
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX && New_arrListContainsX  && New_arrListIndexOfX >=0 && arrList.IndexOf(x) >=0 );
             

        }

    }
}

