Instead, if you need to also test the LastIndexOf method, you might consider using a List<T>. That should give you more test coverage and flexibility in methods you're testing.
Q: Reject the change for an object's property in a custom binding I have a textbox binded to a property that has a length range limitation (max 30 characters). In a WPF custom binding, how can I reject changes to the Text property of the bound object if input text length exceeds 30 characters?
public object Convert(object value, Type targetType, object parameter, CultureInfo culture)
{
    var strValue = value as string;

    // If the string is longer than 30 chars, what else should I do?

    return strValue;
}

public object ConvertBack(object value, Type targetType, object parameter, CultureInfo culture)
{
    var strValue = value as string;

    // If the string is longer than 30 chars, what else should I do?

    return strValue;
}


A: You can add a validate logic:
public object Convert(object value, Type targetType, object parameter, CultureInfo culture)
{
    var strValue = value as string;

    if (strValue != null && strValue.Length > 30)
    {
        // If the string is longer than 30 chars, throw a meaningful exception
        throw new ArgumentException("Text should not exceed 30 characters.");
    }
    return strValue;
}

public object ConvertBack(object value, Type targetType, object parameter, CultureInfo culture)
{
    var strValue = value as string;

    if(strValue != null && strValue.Length > 30)
    {
        // If the string is longer than 30 chars, return a fixed value, or inform user about failure
        return Binding.DoNothing; // This will indicate that binding should do nothing 
    }
    return strValue;
}

This way the binding will reject the incoming data if it's longer than 30 characters.

A: If you want to reject changes to the bound property and not necessarily throw an exception, you can return Binding.DoNothing from ConvertBack:
public object ConvertBack(object value, Type targetType, object parameter, CultureInfo culture)
{
    var strValue = value as string;

    if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(strValue) && strValue.Length > 30)
    {
        // If the string is longer than 30 chars, return DoNothing
        return Binding.DoNothing;
    }

    return strValue;
}

This tells the binding engine to ignore invalid changes.
Please note that this will also get called if the value from the UI is invalid. To react only on valid incoming data, you should add some validation in Convert method as well.
Also please consider handling this situation not only in validation but in your business logic if it possible. It will separate your data presentation layer from business logic, which is good practice. For instance, you could limit text length for display purposes but not reject it from your data objects.

A: You could throw an exception in the ConvertBack method if the user input string length is greater than 30. This invalid input will then be rejected by the binding system, and the data context will remain unmodified by the WPF binding system.
Example:
public object ConvertBack(object value, Type targetType, object parameter, CultureInfo culture)
{
    var strValue = value as string;

    if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(strValue) && strValue.Length > 30)
    {
        throw new ArgumentException("Text must not exceed 30 characters.");
    }

    return strValue;
}

