                (New_Ret < 0 ||
                Old_arrListContainsX &&
                New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX));
        }
    }
}

I have removed the condition from the debug assert where it says "@ If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array list and the returned value is greater or equal to the old index of x". 

What am I missing here?

A: Your test method is not properly structured - it should start with the test initialization and then end with the assertion.

Test initialization:
  
  
*
  
*Assume precondition, initial state and invariant before the operation.
  
  
  Operation:
  
  
*
  
*Do the operation.
  
  
  Postconditions:
  
  
*
  
*Check the postcondition which validates the result of the operation.
  

You are running operations only after you validate the preconditions and do not take any part in the postconidtion phase of the test. 
So if you want to change the test to reflect "@ If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array list and the returned value is greater or equal to the old index of x", you should do so in the "Operation" section or before you run the operation.
Q: Android- How to pass a variable from Fragment to Activity I can pass data between Fragment and Activity using a method like below but can we also pass data between multiple nested fragments?
Example:
From Fragment A to Activity:
Bundle data = new Bundle();
data.putString("key", "Passing variable from Fragment A to Activity");
getActivity().getApplicationContext().sendBroadcast(new Intent().putExtras(data));

From Activity to Fragment A:
@Override
protected void onResume() {
    super.onResume();

    IntentFilter intentFilter = new IntentFilter();
    intentFilter.addAction("ACTION");
    BroadcastReceiver broadcastReceiver = new BroadcastReceiver(){
        @Override
        public void onReceive(Context context, Intent intent){
            Bundle data = intent.getExtras();
            String recv = data.getString("key");
            Log.d("RECEIVED DATA", recv);
        }
    };
    registerReceiver(broadcastReceiver, intentFilter);
}

But I need to pass data from Fragment A to Fragment B.
Is that possible?

A: Yes, you can pass data between fragments. A common way is to use interfaces.
Define an interface in your Fragment B like this:
public interface OnFragmentBListener {
    void onDataPassed(String data);
}

Extend Fragment B from this interface:
public class FragmentB extends Fragment implements OnFragmentBListener {

    @Override
    public void onDataPassed(String data) {
        // use the passed data here
    }
}

In Fragment A you then use this interface to pass data to Fragment B:
((OnFragmentBListener)fragmentBInstance).onDataPassed("Data From A to B");

This code assumes that you have already attached the fragmentBInstance to your activity. 
But, be careful about null-pointers if you are working with the fragment instances.

A: Yes, you can pass data from fragment to another fragment that are nested in the activity.
Here's a basic way by using a listener in the child fragment, as a callback when some event occurs:

1. First, define an interface on your Fragment B like this:

public interface OnDataPassListener {
    void onDataPass(String data);
}

2. In Fragment A use it to pass data to Fragment B:

public class FragmentA extends Fragment {

    OnDataPassListener dataPassListener;

    @Override
    public void onAttach(Context context) { // or onAttach(Activity activity) if you are using the compatibility library
        super.onAttach(context);
        if (context instanceof OnDataPassListener) {
            dataPassListener = (OnDataPassListener) context;
        } else {
            throw new RuntimeException(context.toString() + " must implement OnDataPassListener");
        }
    }

    public void passDataToFragmentB(String data) {
