{% endhighlight %}

The contract test verifies that method `LastIndexOf` behaves as expected, which is if the parameter `x` is found in the original arraylist, `LastIndexOf` should return the highest index of `x`. If `x` does not exist in the arraylist, `LastIndexOf` should return -1.

### PexSetting Attribute
In the contract tests, you can use the PexSetting attribute to specify some specific test situations to be tested or ignored. For instance, we can set the `"StopOnFailure"` as false so that we do not want the tests to stop when a specific failure situation appears. 

{% highlight csharp %}

[PexMethodUnderTest(typeof(AList<>), "LastIndexOf")]
    [PexSetting("StopOnFailure", "False")]
public void TestLastIndexOfFailed(AList<int> aList)
{
    var original = new ArrayList(aList);
    var result = original.LastIndexOf(0);
}

{% endhighlight %}


### Further Practice
Try to write other contract test cases for more methods of ArrayList like AddRange, Clear, InsertRange, RemoveRange, Reverse, Sort...

### Summary

- In Pex, we write test using contract tests that ensure the behavior of methods in the system under test
- We specify properties of input and output states
- We verify the post-conditions of methods to ensure they hold true
- We use PexSetting to specify specific situations of tests
- We generate multiple test cases for random testing

Please remember that there are limitations to Pex tool's test generation capabilities and those who run the generated tests should ensure they cover the specific scenarios they are interested in testing. 

If you encounter any issues, please let me know by opening an issue on the [github page](https://github.com/Microsoft/Pex/issues).

The code examples above are also available on GitHub under the [tests](https://github.com/Microsoft/Pex/tree/master/Examples/ArrayList) directory. They should give you a good starting point for writing your own tests using Pex. 

Let me know if you have any questions by leaving a comment below. Enjoy writing tests with Pex.

[^1]: A `LastIndexOf` will search for the last occurrence of a specific value in an ArrayList.
[^2]: Before running the test, you have to make sure that you have setup the environment correctly to run Pex tests. The setup steps are on documentation page: http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/projects/pex/
[^3]: Contracts make it easier to make assumptions about a system under verification and specify the preconditions, postconditions and invariants of a method or operation.
[^4]: In Pex, the "AssumeUnderTest" attribute is used to indicate that the argument is a parameter of the method being verified. Pex uses this information to reduce the set of tests it generates.
[^5]: The PexObserve.ValueForViewing method is used to output the values of variables which are used in the test to the output panel of the Pex tool. This can be helpful when debugging the tests.
[^6]: You would typically use this type of test to ensure that the behavior of a method meets specified expectations - in this case, that the last index of `x` is what we expect it to be.
[^7]: The 'if' statement with Debug.Assert(...) checks that if 'x' is in the ArrayList, the last index of 'x' is greater than or equal to the value returned by LastIndexOf. Otherwise, the return value is -1.
[^8]: The "StopOnFailure" setting controls whether Pex will stop test generation when the first failure occurs. Set it to "False" to always generate all posible test cases until all are passed or until there are no more combinations to try.
[^9]: An Attribute is a way to give metadata to a class, method, or property so they can be easily discovered and used by the developer. Attributes in C# are used to provide information to the compiler about the element being annotated.--------------------